<TopicPage slug="subjects/earth-science/foundational/minerals-and-crystals">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Pick up any rock and you are holding a handful of minerals — and a mineral is one of the
    most precisely defined objects in natural science: <strong>nature's chemical compounds
    with a structural discipline</strong>. Not "a pretty stone", but a substance that passes
    five tests: it occurs <strong>naturally</strong> (lab-grown rubies are not minerals), it
    is <strong>inorganic</strong>, it is <strong>solid</strong>, its atoms are arranged in an
    ordered, repeating <strong>crystalline lattice</strong>, and it has a
    <strong>definite chemical composition</strong> (or a composition varying within defined
    limits). Those five criteria do real work. Coal fails (no order in its atoms); glass
    fails (a frozen liquid, no lattice); petroleum fails (liquid, mixed); a lab diamond fails
    the first test — even though it is chemically identical to the mineral. Learning to read
    a mineral — hardness, cleavage, lustre — is learning chemistry you can do with a pocket
    knife, a copper coin and a streak plate.
  </p>
  <p>
    Why does a geologist care so much about the ingredients of rocks? Because minerals are
    the alphabet: every rock is a sentence built from them, every mountain range a library,
    and every metal in your phone — copper for wires, lithium for the battery, indium for
    the screen — was once a mineral concentration that had to be found, understood and mined.
    This lesson covers the syllabus's five topics: the chemistry, the formation, the great
    silicate family, the non-silicates, and the identification tests that turn a hand
    specimen into a name.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the recipe, the lattice, the diagnostic tests, the families" widget="equationStep" config={mineralCfg} />

  <h2>The chemistry of minerals</h2>
  <p>
    The lattice is the heart of the definition, and it explains the subject's most famous
    example. <strong>Diamond and graphite are both pure carbon</strong> — same element,
    different <em>arrangement</em> — and the arrangement changes everything. Diamond bonds
    each carbon to four neighbours in a rigid 3-D framework, the hardest natural substance
    (Mohs 10) and an electrical insulator. Graphite bonds carbons in flat sheets held to the
    next sheet by weak forces: it peels onto paper (pencil "lead"), slides as a lubricant,
    and conducts electricity along its sheets (Mohs 1–2). Minerals with the same chemistry
    but different structure are called <strong>polymorphs</strong>, and the phenomenon runs
    deep — quartz has several pressure-dependent polymorphs used to reconstruct how deep a
    rock was buried. The recipe discipline also allows controlled substitution: olivine's
    formula is (Mg,Fe)₂SiO₄ — magnesium and iron swap freely in the same lattice, and the
    swap records the temperature of formation. Roughly 6,000 mineral species are named,
    each a specific structure-chemistry pairing, and the International Mineralogical
    Association polices the list — a new species claim needs a crystal structure solution,
    not just a pretty crystal.
  </p>

  <h2>How minerals form</h2>
  <p>
    Crystals grow whenever the right elements can find each other under the right
    conditions, and there are four main birthplaces. <strong>From a melt:</strong> as magma
    cools, minerals crystallise in sequence — olivine first (~1,200 °C), quartz last — and
    the <strong>cooling rate sets the crystal size</strong>: slow cooling deep underground
    gives atoms time to stack into large, well-formed crystals (granite's grains); fast
    cooling at the surface freezes microscopic ones (basalt's); instantly (obsidian)
    freezes none at all — glass fails the lattice test, which is exactly why obsidian is
    not a mineral. <strong>From solution:</strong> as water evaporates or cools, dissolved
    ions precipitate — halite and gypsum in evaporating basins, cave calcite from dripping
    carbonate water. <strong>From hot fluids:</strong> hydrothermal waters percolating
    through cracks deposit quartz veins and metal ores (much of the world's copper, gold
    and silver). <strong>By transformation:</strong> existing minerals recrystallise under
    heat and pressure — limestone becomes marble, shale's clays become mica — without
    melting, the mechanism of the metamorphic world.
  </p>

  <h2>Crystal form: the shape of the lattice</h2>
  <p>
    When a crystal grows unimpeded, its outward faces take on shapes from a limited
    menu: <strong>seven crystal systems</strong> — cubic (halite, pyrite, garnet),
    tetragonal, orthorhombic, monoclinic, triclinic, hexagonal (quartz's six-sided
    prisms) and trigonal (calcite's rhombs) — each a distinct symmetry of the repeating
    lattice. The internal order explains the external habit: halite's cubic cleavage
    and cubic crystals are the same cubic lattice seen at two scales; pyrite grows
    natural cubes and near-perfect icosahedral-looking pyritohedra; quartz's hexagonal
    columns with slanted terminations are its framework's fingerprint. Habit varies
    with growth conditions — the same calcite can form rhombs, needles or scalenohedra
    ("dogtooth") — so form is a clue, not an ID card; the lattice is the invariant,
    and X-ray diffraction (Going deeper) is what reads it directly.
  </p>

  <h2>The silicates: Earth's default mineral</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Silicates make up more than 90% of the crust</strong>, and they are all
    variations on one building block: the silicon–oxygen <strong>tetrahedron</strong>
    (SiO₄⁴⁻), a pyramid of one silicon and four oxygens. Everything else is architecture —
    how the tetrahedra link. <strong>Isolated</strong> tetrahedra bonded by iron and
    magnesium give <strong>olivine</strong>, dense and green, the dominant mineral of the
    upper mantle. <strong>Single chains</strong> give <strong>pyroxene</strong>;
    <strong>double chains</strong> give <strong>amphibole</strong> (the chain linkage leaves
    oxygen charges to balance, so these take in calcium, sodium, aluminium — complexity
    increases as linkage does). <strong>Sheets</strong> — tetrahedra tiled into planes —
    give the <strong>micas</strong> (muscovite, biotite): perfect single-sheet cleavage,
    the mineral that peels into transparent windows. <strong>Frameworks</strong> — every
    oxygen shared between tetrahedra — give <strong>feldspar</strong> (the most abundant
    mineral family in the crust, its pink and white grains in granite) and
    <strong>quartz</strong>, the pure-SiO₂ end member: no charge to balance, nothing to
    bond, chemically stubborn — which is why quartz survives weathering and ends up as
    every beach sand. The whole sequence is also a thermometer: Bowen's reaction series
    orders it by crystallisation temperature, so a rock's mineral lineup tells you the
    story of its cooling.
  </p>

  <h2>The non-silicates: small in mass, mighty in value</h2>
  <p>
    The other mineral classes are crustal minorities but carry most of the economy.
    <strong>Carbonates</strong> (calcite CaCO₃, dolomite) build limestone, marble and most
    shells; calcite's party trick — fizzing with dilute acid — is the standard field test.
    <strong>Oxides</strong> (hematite Fe₂O₃, magnetite Fe₃O₄, rutile TiO₂) are the iron
    and titanium ores: hematite's rust-red streak paints every old mine trail, magnetite
    is the natural magnet. <strong>Sulfides</strong> (pyrite FeS₂ — "fool's gold" —
    galena PbS, chalcopyrite CuFeS₂) carry copper, lead, zinc, silver: metallic, dense,
    and formed where hot fluids met reducing conditions. <strong>Halides</strong>
    (halite = table salt) and <strong>sulfates</strong> (gypsum of plaster and drywall)
    are the evaporite salts. <strong>Native elements</strong> — gold, silver, copper,
    sulfur, carbon (as diamond or graphite) — occur uncombined, and gold's native form is
    precisely why it was humanity's first metal: you can find it shining in a streambed,
    already pure. Note the pattern for economic geology: <strong>ores are unusual mineral
    concentrations</strong> — banded iron formations are fossilised ancient ocean
    chemistry, porphyry coppers are hydrothermal plumbing systems, lithium brines are
    evaporated basins. Every battery and wind turbine starts as one of these.
  </p>

  <h2>Identifying minerals: the field toolkit</h2>
  <p>
    No lab required — the diagnostic properties narrow 6,000 candidates to one in a
    handful of tests. <strong>Hardness</strong> runs on the <strong>Mohs scale</strong>
    (1812), a comparative ladder from talc (1) to diamond (10): fingernail ~2.5, copper
    coin ~3.5, steel knife ~5.5, window glass ~5.5, quartz 7. Scratching tells you where a
    specimen sits between reference points — a mineral your knife cannot scratch but
    quartz can is between 5.5 and 7. <strong>Cleavage</strong> is how a mineral breaks
    along its lattice's weak planes: mica in perfect single sheets, halite in cubes
    (three planes at 90°), calcite in rhombs (three planes not at 90°), quartz in none —
    it <em>fractures</em> into curved conchoidal shells instead. Cleavage and fracture
    are separate observations: cleavage is internal architecture; fracture is failure.
    <strong>Lustre</strong> sorts metallic from glassy, pearly, silky, earthy.
    <strong>Streak</strong> — the powder colour when scraped on unglazed porcelain — is
    more reliable than the specimen's surface colour: hematite looks black, silver or red
    but always streaks rust-red. <strong>Density</strong> separates the heavies: galena
    (7.5 g/cm³) feels twice as heavy as quartz (2.65). Special tests finish the job:
    calcite fizzes in dilute HCl; magnetite deflects a compass; halite tastes of salt
    (yes, geologists do).
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Run the toolkit once, end to end, to see the narrowing. An unknown specimen: glassy
    lustre, colourless, no cleavage but curved conchoidal fracture, scratches glass and
    a steel knife (hardness > 5.5) but not a quartz crystal (&lt; 7), streaks white, density
    2.65, no fizz in acid. The tests have eliminated all malleable metallic minerals,
    everything softer than 5.5, everything with cleavage, all carbonates (no fizz), and
    the density rules out beryl (2.7–2.9) and feldspar (2.55–2.75 is close — but
    feldspar's two good cleavages are absent). What survives is <strong>quartz</strong>
    — and the identification took four observations. This is the discipline of mineral
    identification: not memorising 6,000 species, but knowing the ~30 common
    rock-formers cold and letting the tests do the elimination.
  </p>
  <p>
    Minerals are also <strong>records</strong>. The chemistry and inclusions of a crystal
    record the pressure, temperature and fluids of its growth, and the record can be dated:
    <strong>zircon</strong> (ZrSiO₄) incorporates uranium but rejects lead when it grows, so
    every lead atom inside is radiogenic decay — a clock started at crystallisation. Zircon
    crystals from Western Australia dated at 4.4 billion years are the oldest known material
    from Earth, and their oxygen-isotope chemistry implies liquid water existed startlingly
    early in the Hadean — a planet's entire early history read from a sand-grain-sized
    mineral. The same logic (and the same mineral) dates the oldest lunar and Martian grains.
  </p>
  <p>
    The deeper science is <strong>crystallography</strong>. That the outward habit mirrors
    the inner lattice was guessed for centuries, but proven when von Laue (1912) and the
    Braggs father-and-son team (Nobel 1915) fired X-rays through crystals and read the
    diffraction: crystals are diffraction gratings with atomic spacing. X-ray
    crystallography then became the way matter is seen — the structures of table salt,
    penicillin, insulin, and finally DNA (Franklin's Photo 51, 1952) were all solved by
    shining X-rays through crystals — a technique born from geology that restructured
    chemistry, physics and biology in turn. Gems close the loop between science and value:
    diamond (carbon at mantle pressures), ruby (chromium impurity in corundum — the same
    mineral as sapphire, coloured differently), emerald (beryl with chromium/vanadium) —
    value tracks the rarity of the <em>conditions</em>, not just hardness. And the frontier
    keeps moving: "mineral evolution" studies how the mineral kingdom itself grew from ~a
    dozen pre-solar species to thousands, as biology — oxygen-producing life above all —
    gave the planet new chemistry to crystallise.
  </p>
